{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788222665,"nonce":"cfm3mYqWIMfpwvh6IoH11pF/Ui0=","message":"SECOND OPEN QUESTION RESOLVED — Evan, 2026-08-31 (session 10).\n\n**M1 must demonstrate the FULL draft → fight → undraft cycle, and a completed\nundraft is a HARD ACCEPTANCE CRITERION, not a report line.**\n\nThe reasoning, recorded so it is not re-litigated: a drafted colonist does not\neat, sleep or work, and NO ALERT NAMES IT. Over ten days, forgetting to undraft\nafter one raid is a slow-motion wipe that looks, right up until it doesn't, like\nnothing went wrong. That is precisely the silent failure class M1 exists to\ncatch, so grading it as a report would let a known-broken cycle ship as passed.\n\nThis closes the procedural hole raised on 4.2 and repeated in the session-4\namendment here: the emergency posture DRAFTS AND NEVER UNDRAFTS. Making undraft\npass/fail is what forces it shut.\n\nNote the interaction with SeekAndKill, already confirmed at source:\n`SeekRegistry.ShouldSeek` requires `!pawn.Drafted`, so a colonist left drafted is\nalso removed from the seek posture — the two failure modes compound rather than\nmerely coexist.\n\nAdd to this issue's Acceptance: the run's transcript shows draft, engagement, and\nundraft for every colonist drafted during the raid, and the final digest reads 0\ndrafted with 0 hostiles.","files":null}]}